# Editing Basics - File > New Scene, then type a spinning triangle ringed
# by a torus, committing each line with `;` - the same keystrokes a user
# would make. Along the way: code focus, cursor-driven overlays,
# autocomplete, and the right-click GL-state inspector.
#
# Points are symbolic targets (grammar: src/app/glr_pointer_script.h),
# resolved against the live layout at fire time. `code:<n>` is a document
# row, 1-based; the panel gutter shows it one higher because row 1 of the
# gutter is the generated `void display(void) {`.
#
# The caption band anchors at scene:0.25,0.76 - inside the scene viewport,
# above the status bar, and clear of the variable panel's right column.

move scene:0.55,0.30
echo bitmap scene:0.25,0.76 24 3.2 Let's build a scene from scratch.
pause 2.6

# A brand-new scene (File > New Scene natively, the shell's New button on web)
#ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__
glide shell:new 0.8             # browser shell's New button
click shell:new
glide scene:0.55,0.30 0.6       # bring the tour cursor back into the canvas
#else
glide menu:file 0.8             # the File menu
click
glide item:new_scene 0.5        # New Scene row
click
#endif
chord ctrl+shift c              # recenter the camera

echo bitmap scene:0.25,0.76 24 3.6 A new scene seeds the GL boilerplate.
glide code:3 0.4
ring  code:1 0.3
ring  code:2 0.3
ring  code:3 0.3
ring  code:4 0.3
ring  code:5 0.3
ring  code:6 0.3
pause 1.2

# @checkpoint after-new-scene

# Code focus - the panel shows your lines only; turning it off reveals the
# generated C that the scene exports as. Ctrl+Shift+F, or the "focus" keycap
# in the status bar.
echo bitmap scene:0.25,0.76 24 3.6 Those six lines are all the panel shows.
pause 3.0
echo bitmap scene:0.25,0.76 24 3.6 Ctrl+Shift+F turns code focus off.
chord ctrl+shift f              # follow-scroll keeps your rows in view
pause 3.4
echo bitmap scene:0.25,0.76 18 5.5 Camera, lights and the display() frame wrap those rows -\nthis is the C the scene exports as, generated for you.
pause 5.5
echo bitmap scene:0.25,0.76 24 3.4 Ctrl+Shift+F again - back to just your code.
chord ctrl+shift f
pause 3.0

echo bitmap scene:0.25,0.76 24 3.4 Type a command; the ; key commits it.

# A comment line, then the spin - \cj is Enter
key \cj
key@20 // Let's create a rotating triangle surrounded by a torus
key ;

key@10 glRotatef(
pause 0.2
key@5 t * 40, 0, 1, 0);

# The cursor row drives the overlays: park it on glRotatef and the scene
# shows what that transform is doing.
echo bitmap scene:0.25,0.76 18 8.0 The cursor position also drives the overlays: with the cursor\non the glRotatef line, its effect on the geometry is revealed.
skey up
glide code:9 0.5
ring code:9 1.5
glide scene:0.5,0.5 1.0
ring scene:0.5,0.5 1.5
pause 3.0
skey down

key@10 glBegin(GL_TRIANGLES)
key ;

# @checkpoint before-geometry

key@10 glColor3f(1, 0.4, 0.4)
key ;

# The vertex overlay narrows as each coordinate is typed: plane, line, point.
echo bitmap scene:0.25,0.76 24 4.0 Overlays also guide you as a vertex is specified:
key@10 glVertex3f(-1,
echo bitmap scene:0.25,0.76 24 4.0 Overlays also guide you as a vertex is specified:\n2 degrees of freedom left - the plane x = -1.
ring scene:0.5,0.5 2.0
pause 2.0
key@10 -0.7,
echo bitmap scene:0.25,0.76 24 4.0 Overlays also guide you as a vertex is specified:\n1 degree of freedom left - the line x = -1, y = -0.7.
pause 4.0
key@10 0)
echo bitmap scene:0.25,0.76 24 4.0 Overlays also guide you as a vertex is specified:\n0 degrees of freedom left - the point (-1, -0.7, 0).
pause 4.0
key ;

echo bitmap scene:0.25,0.76 24 3.6 Now the other two corners of the triangle.
key@20 glColor3f(0.4, 1, 0.4)
key ;
key@20 glVertex3f(1, -0.7, 0)
key ;
key@20 glColor3f(0.4, 0.4, 1)
key ;
key@20 glVertex3f(0, 1.1, 0)
key ;
key@20 glEnd()
key ;
echo bitmap scene:0.25,0.76 24 3.4 One triangle, three colors, spinning.
pause 3.4

# @checkpoint before-torus

# Autocomplete: type a prefix, let the ghost show the rest.
echo bitmap scene:0.25,0.76 24 3.6 The gray ghost is autocomplete (Tab).
key \cj
key@20 glutSolidT
pause 2.6                       # let the autocomplete ghost linger
key@20 orus(0.25, 1.4, 16, 32)
key ;
skey up

echo bitmap scene:0.25,0.76 24 5.0 The torus inherits the last glColor,\nas the gutter swatch shows.
glide code:15 1.0
ring code:15 1.5
pause 2.5
echo bitmap scene:0.25,0.76 24 4.0 It inherits the transforms above it too.
glide code:9 1.0
ring code:9 1.5
pause 2.5

# @checkpoint before-gl-state

# Right-click a blank row for the GL state at that point in the program.
echo bitmap scene:0.25,0.76 24 5.0 Right-click a blank row to read the GL state\nat that point in the program.
glide code:18 1.0
rightclick
pause 4.5
echo bitmap scene:0.25,0.76 24 5.0 Shift + right-click a second blank row to compare\nthe two points instead of the GL defaults.
glide code:7 1.0
rightclick shift
pause 5.0

echo bitmap scene:0.25,0.76 24 4.0 Tour finished - it's all yours!\nEscape takes control now.
